y Wollstonecraft","confidence":0.9},{"name":"Mary Shelley","confidence":0.85}],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The book argues that Mary Wollstonecraft and Mary Shelley—though they did not share their lives—together left a powerful literary and feminist legacy shaped by their resistance to women’s injustices and their decision to live in exile.","confidence":0.72},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.35},"profileConfidence":0.56,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"47160b00e98826378ae35c1b2c7df0494988397474bf83727dbdeb2675c7715d","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Biography","secondarySubjects":["Gender & Sexuality","Arts & Criticism"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book focuses on the intertwined lives of Mary Wollstonecraft and Mary Shelley, presented as a biographical account with significant themes of gender and literary contributions.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"d32ff1ba3334f029624ba4fbff74c6128db52742b596ffade8dc23f5a5680cdd","summary":"NATIONAL BOOK CRITICS CIRCLE AWARD WINNER • NAMED ONE OF THE BEST BOOKS OF THE YEAR BY THE SEATTLE TIMES This groundbreaking dual biography brings to life a pioneering English feminist and the daughter she never knew. Mary Wollstonecraft and Mary Shelley have each been the subject of numerous biographies, yet no one has ever examined their lives in one book—until now. In Romantic Outlaws, Charlotte Gordon reunites the trailblazing author who wrote A Vindication of the Rights of Woman and the Romantic visionary who gave the world Frankenstein—two courageous women who should have shared their lives, but instead shared a powerful literary and feminist legacy. In 1797, less than two weeks after giving birth to her second daughter, Mary Wollstonecraft died, and a remarkable life spent pushing against the boundaries of society’s expectations for women came to an end. But another was just beginning. Wollstonecraft’s daughter Mary was to follow a similarly audacious path. Both women had passionate relationships with several men, bore children out of wedlock, and chose to live in exile outside their native country. Each in her own time fought against the injustices women faced and wrote books that changed literary history.","displaySummary":"In Romantic Outlaws, Charlotte Gordon reunites the trailblazing author who wrote A Vindication of the Rights of Woman and the Romantic visionary who gave the world Frankenstein—two courageous women who should have shared their lives, but instead shared a powerful literary and feminist legacy. In 1797, less than two weeks after giving birth to her second daughter, Mary Wollstonecraft died, and a remarkable life spent pushing against the boundaries of society’s expectations for women came to an end. But another was just beginning. Wollstonecraft’s daughter Mary was to follow a similarly audacious path. Both women had passionate relationships with several men, bore children out of wedlock, and chose to live in exile outside their native country. Each in her own time fought against the injustices women faced and wrote books that changed literary history.","primaryTopic":"Family Biography","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Biography","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Biography","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Biography","mappedSubject":"Biography","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book focuses on the intertwined lives of Mary Wollstonecraft and Mary Shelley, presented as a biographical account with significant themes of gender and literary contributions.","id":"subject-evidence-book-romantic-outlaws-the-extraordinary-lives-of-mary-wollstonecraft-and-her-daughter-mary-shelley-ch-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"BIOGRAPHY & AUTOBIOGRAPHY","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiograp